    "author_association": "NONE",
    "body": "### Contributing guidelines\n\n- [X] I have read [CONTRIBUTING.md](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/main/CONTRIBUTING.md)\n- [X] I have read [CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/main/CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md)\n- [X] I have updated 'mini.nvim' to latest version\n\n### Module(s)\n\nai\n\n### Description\n\nwhen executing an `inside` or `around` command with mini.ai configured with a `search_method` of `cover_or_next`, the next pair is chosen instead of the covering pair. This happens when the closing delimiter of the covering pair is on another line.\n\n### Neovim version\n\n0.7.0\n\n### Steps to reproduce\n\n1. `nvim -nu minimal.lua`\r\n2. `:lua require(\"mini.ai\").setup({search_method = \"cover_or_next\"})`\r\n3. Type the following in an empty buffer\r\n```\r\n(if (blah)\r\n)\r\n```\r\n4. Put your cursor at the first column of the first line\r\n5. `dab`\n\n### Expected behavior\n\nThe whole thing should be deleted and you should be left with an empty buffer.  (this is the behavior of native `dab`)\n\n### Actual behavior\n\nThe `next` match gets deleted\r\n```\r\n(if \r\n)\r\n```",
